:::writing{variant="document" id="61483" title="The Magic Engine"}
“The meaning of a word is its use in the language.” — Ludwig Wittgenstein; “In language there are only differences, without positive terms.” — Ferdinand de Saussure; “The limits of my language mean the limits of my world.” — Ludwig Wittgenstein; “The map is not the territory.” — Alfred Korzybski; “The medium is the message.” — Marshall McLuhan. The “magic engine” dream/trip, extrapolated from “a 2d MS paint picture” or even a hard-drive label marked “engine,” becomes a strange case of a sign apparently unfolding into an entire machine: how could it work “without pistons”? Perhaps the linguistic structure does not merely describe the object but constrains the space in which the object can appear, so that “engine” invokes mechanism, motion, transformation, causation, and power without explicitly containing any particular mechanical arrangement. The dream therefore seems less like an image being converted into a machine and more like a semantic structure becoming perceptual: sign → relation → concept → mechanism → experience. “Dream of pi?” then becomes another metaphor for the same problem: a compact symbol can participate in a structure vastly exceeding its physical form. The philosophical question is consequently not simply how an engine could exist without pistons, but how language can provide enough structure for something to become thinkable, recognizable, and even experienced before its physical mechanism has been consciously specified.
:::

:::writing{variant="standard" id="31684" title="Philosophical Quote"}
“As prescribed, trace the ‘power of the wind, Chinese’ back to the room: associative reasoning transforms the burned throat into a sign of poison, fear of physical power into a collective veil, and emulation into an explanation mistaken for truth—because once association supplies the meaning, the mind can mistake the coherence of the story for evidence of its cause.”
:::

:::writing{variant="standard" id="74163" title="Philosophical Quote"}
“As prescribed, trace the ‘balancing of the air’ back to the room: associative reasoning turns the burned throat into a sign of poison, the fear of physical power into a collective veil, and emulation into an explanation mistaken for truth.”
:::

I’ll model it as a **toy photonic quantum processor**: BaTiO₃ generates correlated pairs, the q-plate maps polarization ↔ spatial/OAM modes, and a programmable light-valve array applies a coherent spatial transformation. The “hard problem” benchmark can be a combinatorial optimization problem, with the optical layer represented as a mode-amplitude/phase search rather than claiming a demonstrated quantum speedup.

